    "body": "Merck plans to invest $2.9 billion to expand vaccine and medicine manufacturing and drug research in Montgomery County, Pennsylvania, by the end of 2030. State officials announced the news on Wednesday as the company opened a new research facility at its West Point site.\nWhat’s Related\nThe $2.9 billion investment includes $550 million for the new ADVANCE facility at West Point and research facilities at nearby Spring House Innovation Park. Pennsylvania has also proposed a $5 million grant for the first West Point projects through its Redevelopment Assistance Capital Program.\nGov. Josh Shapiro and state economic development officials joined Merck leaders for the ADVANCE ribbon-cutting. The opening adds an automated laboratory to a site that already manufactures more than 200 million vaccine doses each year, according to the state.\n“Merck’s major investment in Montgomery County is another major win for the Commonwealth,” said Shapiro.\nRELATED STORIES: Merck to Build $3 Billion Manufacturing Facility in Virginia | Lilly Announces Plans For $3.5B Manufacturing Plant in Pennsylvania\nRobots and AI for clinical research\nThe 120,000-square-foot ADVANCE facility uses robotics , automation, and AI to help analyze biological samples from Merck’s clinical development programs. That work helps researchers evaluate medicines and vaccines being tested in clinical trials.\n“The ADVANCE facility represents the future of clinical development at Merck,” said Rob Davis, Merck’s chairman and CEO.\nMerck says the laboratory will automate data handling and analysis to deliver results faster. It also expects automation to reduce human error and free scientists from routine tasks so they can spend more time on other research.\nThe company says its systems are designed to work together and adapt as laboratory technology changes. The laboratory will also use information management systems to organize samples, testing and research data.\nA major vaccine manufacturing site\nWest Point covers 400 acres and is Merck’s largest combined research and human health manufacturing site. In addition to making and testing pharmaceuticals , vaccines, and biological products, it serves as a distribution center for other U.S. production sites.\nPennsylvania economic development Secretary Rick Siger said the state funding would help projects that “advance the development and manufacturing of critical medicines and vaccines.”\nMerck acquired West Point through its 1953 merger with Sharp & Dohme. The company says it has invested more than $15 billion in the site over the past 35 years.\nThat construction spending has sustained an on-site workforce of about 800 people in contracting, engineering and construction jobs over that period, Merck said. The company also says many projects were designed in Pennsylvania, generating more than $1 billion in services for local architects.\nThe $550 million research investment at ADVANCE and Spring House is part of Merck’s previously announced $70 billion commitment to U.S. manufacturing and research.",
